    categories: ["geopolitica"],
    title: "Cape Verde's President Neves projected to win re-election with 64% of vote",
    summary: "Preliminary results, based on 98% of the ballots counted, show that the incumbent president and candidate for re-election, José Maria Neves, has won this presidential election with nearly 64% of the vote, while his opponent, Joana Rosa, received 31.5%.",
    preview: "Cape Verde With 98 percent of votes counted, Cape Verde’s President José Maria Neves is projected to win re-election with nearly 64 percent of the vote. Claiming victory on Sunday evening, Neves promised to build cross-party support to improve housing, healthcare and education, and create jobs. “I am going to be a president as we affirmed in the electoral campaign and as we carried out in the first term, of all Cabo Verdeans, independently of their political and social sensibilities,\" Neves told supporters.",
    body: "Cape Verde\n\nWith 98 percent of votes counted, Cape Verde’s President José Maria Neves is projected to win re-election with nearly 64 percent of the vote.\n\nClaiming victory on Sunday evening, Neves promised to build cross-party support to improve housing, healthcare and education, and create jobs.\n\n“I am going to be a president as we affirmed in the electoral campaign and as we carried out in the first term, of all Cabo Verdeans, independently of their political and social sensibilities,\" Neves told supporters.\n\n\"The president of this republic is not about the majority vote, the president of the republic is for everyone and you can count on this president to forge the union of all Cabo Verdeans in the islands and in the diaspora.”\n\nOfficial results show turnout at around 32 percent of the more than 400,000 eligible voters.\n\nThe country’s first ever female presidential candidate Joana Rosa came second with 31.5 percent.\n\nThe West African archipelago has a history of competitive elections and peaceful transfers of power. It is considered a model of democracy in Africa, with strong economic growth.\n\nThe African Party for the Independence of Cape Verde, which backs Neves and controls the government and most municipalities won a parliamentary majority in May, ending a decade of rule by the Movement for Democracy.\n\nUnder Cape Verde’s parliamentary system, the prime minister directs policy and holds most executive power. The directly elected president’s role is largely ceremonial, although the office carries the power to veto legislation.\n\nNeves was first elected president in 2021. From 2001 to 2016 he also served as prime minister and president of the African Party for the Independence of Cape Verde (PAICV). He has also been a mayor and a lawmaker.\n\nIn July, the World Bank classified Cape Verde as an upper-middle-income country thanks to economic growth in the tourism sector.\n\nHowever, the country faces many challenges, including persistent social inequality, geographic isolation and a lack of affordable options for travelling between islands. Among the population's major concerns are the increased cost of living, fuel prices and youth unemployment.",
