    body: "Six months into the biggest Ebola outbreak in the Democratic Republic of Congo’s history, containing the virus increasingly depends on solving problems far beyond Ebola — hunger, armed conflict, mistrust and a health system already buckling under the strain.\n\nMore than 11,000 people have been infected and 5,500 have died since the outbreak was declared May 15. For months, Ebola has been killing roughly 240 people a week, the equivalent of about one and a half Boeing Co. 737 passenger jets crashing every week.\n\n“It’s a systems problem compounded by basically geography, insecurity, conflict, poor roads, mistrust,” said William Clemmer, a physician who has worked in Congo for three decades and is senior regional health adviser for Corus International based in Goma.",
